WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.211 --> 00:00:09.154
Hamlet thought about what to do next as he watched musicians gathering in the great hall. Soon Rosencrantz and Guildenstern returned.

2
00:00:09.156 --> 00:00:15.290
"Sir," said Guildenstern to Hamlet, "the king is angry and your mother is very upset."

3
00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:20.640
"Perhaps you'd feel better," added Rosencrantz, "if you told us what's troubling you."

4
00:00:20.641 --> 00:00:24.394
Hamlet was certain now that his friends were spying on him.

5
00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:27.434
He grabbed a flute from one of the musicians.

6
00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:30.061
"Can you play this?" he said.

7
00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:34.871
"I cannot get a sound out of it," replied Guildenstern nervously.

8
00:00:34.872 --> 00:00:41.810
"And yet you think that you can get sounds out of me—that I'll tell you what's on my mind!" exclaimed Hamlet.

9
00:00:41.811 --> 00:00:45.839
"You are wrong to think I am easier to play than a flute!"

10
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.704
Rosencrantz and Guildenstern scurried out just as Polonius entered.

11
00:00:50.705 --> 00:00:54.545
"Your mother wishes to see you, my lord," the old man said.

12
00:00:54.546 --> 00:00:57.766
Then Polonius went to Gertrude's sitting room.

13
00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:05.410
"When Hamlet gets here, try to find out what's really bothering him," he instructed and hid himself behind a heavy curtain.

14
00:01:05.411 --> 00:01:09.736
Meanwhile Rosencrantz and Guildenstern had gone straight to the king.

15
00:01:09.737 --> 00:01:16.950
They reported that Hamlet seemed even more deranged than before. When they left, the king was all alone.

16
00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:24.379
"What have I done?" he thought. "My sin is the greatest of all—I've killed my own brother.

17
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:32.603
How can I be forgiven, since I am not willing to give up what I have gained by my foul deed—my brother's kingdom and his queen?

18
00:01:32.604 --> 00:01:40.532
Angels in heaven, soften this heart of steel and help me repent!" He knelt down and tried to pray.

19
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:46.247
At that moment Hamlet walked past Claudius' chambers and saw the king kneeling.

20
00:01:46.248 --> 00:01:51.460
"I could kill him now so easily!" he thought, drawing his sword.

21
00:01:51.461 --> 00:02:00.602
Then he paused. "Claudius murdered my father before my father had time to confess his sins, so he could not go straight to heaven.

22
00:02:00.603 --> 00:02:08.518
But if I kill Claudius now, while he's praying, this murderer will go to heaven! What kind of revenge is that?

23
00:02:08.519 --> 00:02:15.350
It's better to kill him when he's drinking or gambling—when his soul is black and headed straight to hell!"

24
00:02:15.351 --> 00:02:19.443
Putting his sword back in its sheath, Hamlet quietly left.

25
00:02:19.444 --> 00:02:25.778
Claudius sighed and stood up. "My words are empty," he muttered.

26
00:02:25.779 --> 00:02:31.373
"My heart is not in them. And empty words can never reach God's ear."

27
00:02:31.374 --> 00:02:37.372
When Hamlet got to the queen's chambers, Gertrude said, "You have offended the king, your father."

28
00:02:37.373 --> 00:02:43.111
"Mother," replied Hamlet, "you have offended the king, my father."

29
00:02:43.112 --> 00:02:45.658
"How dare you speak to me this way?"

30
00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:50.437
Gertrude began to get up from her chair, but Hamlet pushed her back down.

31
00:02:50.438 --> 00:02:54.441
"You shall not budge until we talk this out!" he shouted.

32
00:02:54.442 --> 00:03:00.100
Frightened by Hamlet's roughness, the queen cried out, "Do you mean to kill me? Help!"

33
00:03:00.101 --> 00:03:07.171
Polonius, who could hear but not see what was happening, panicked. "Help, help!" he yelled.

34
00:03:07.172 --> 00:03:14.759
Hamlet, thinking that Polonius was Claudius, thrust his sword through the curtain and stabbed the old man.

35
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.298
Gertrude gasped. "What a rash and bloody deed!"

36
00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:24.640
"Almost as bloody as killing a king and marrying his brother!" said Hamlet.

37
00:03:24.641 --> 00:03:28.505
"Killing a king?" echoed Gertrude, bewildered.

38
00:03:28.506 --> 00:03:32.298
Hamlet lifted the curtain and saw Polonius' body.

39
00:03:32.299 --> 00:03:35.553
"Farewell, you old, meddling fool.

40
00:03:35.554 --> 00:03:39.609
Being a busybody can be dangerous," he said.

41
00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:50.815
"Now, Mother. My father was handsome, noble, kind—how could you dishonor his memory by so hastily marrying that villain and murderer?"

42
00:03:50.816 --> 00:03:54.830
The queen went pale. "Oh Hamlet, stop!"

43
00:03:54.831 --> 00:04:00.434
At that moment the ghost appeared to the prince. Hamlet jumped up, staring.

44
00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:04.537
"What do you want? Do you come to scold me for taking too long?"

45
00:04:04.538 --> 00:04:08.673
"You must avenge me," wailed the ghost.

46
00:04:08.674 --> 00:04:12.703
"Who are you speaking to?" Gertrude whispered.

47
00:04:12.704 --> 00:04:15.362
"My father's spirit! Can't you see it?"

48
00:04:15.363 --> 00:04:20.428
"Alas, you are truly mad!" his mother moaned.

49
00:04:20.429 --> 00:04:24.894
The ghost vanished, and Hamlet looked down at the dead Polonius.

50
00:04:24.895 --> 00:04:29.688
"For this I am sorry," he muttered. "Mother, good night."

51
00:04:29.689 --> 00:04:37.597
After Hamlet left, Gertrude rushed to Claudius and told him that Hamlet had killed Polonius in a fit of madness.

52
00:04:37.598 --> 00:04:44.018
The king was now very afraid for his own life. He summoned Rosencrantz and Guildenstern.

53
00:04:44.019 --> 00:04:47.764
"Hamlet has committed a grave crime," said the king.

54
00:04:47.765 --> 00:04:52.113
"You must take him to England. There is a boat leaving soon."

55
00:04:52.114 --> 00:04:58.312
He handed Rosencrantz a letter. "Present this to the king of England on your arrival."

56
00:04:58.313 --> 00:05:04.820
What no one but the king knew was that the letter contained orders to have Hamlet put to death!

57
00:05:04.821 --> 00:05:10.058
That night Rosencrantz, Guildenstern, and Hamlet rode toward the coast.

58
00:05:10.059 --> 00:05:14.606
On their way they saw thousands of soldiers marching along the road.

59
00:05:14.607 --> 00:05:18.351
Hamlet stopped their captain and inquired who they were.

60
00:05:18.352 --> 00:05:22.645
"We are the army of Norway, sir," replied the captain.

61
00:05:22.646 --> 00:05:27.614
"Your king gave us permission to march through Denmark on our way to Poland."

62
00:05:27.615 --> 00:05:32.336
"Prince Fortinbras is waging war against Poland?" said Hamlet.

63
00:05:32.337 --> 00:05:35.311
"Not really," answered the captain.

64
00:05:35.312 --> 00:05:41.328
"He wants to capture only a tiny piece of land, which the Poles will fight to defend."

65
00:05:41.329 --> 00:05:51.288
Hamlet was astonished. "Fortinbras has put together a whole army," he thought, "and will risk his life for a small patch of earth.

66
00:05:51.289 --> 00:05:57.987
I have a beloved father's murder to avenge, and I've done nothing! Fortinbras shames me!"

67
00:05:57.988 --> 00:06:02.011
At that moment something changed inside Hamlet.

68
00:06:02.012 --> 00:06:07.628
He resolved to find a way to return to Elsinore and kill Claudius.

69
00:06:07.629 --> 00:06:14.580
"From this time forth," he said to himself, "my thoughts will be either bloody or worthless!"

70
00:06:14.581 --> 00:06:22.056
But for now, with Rosencrantz and Guildenstern as guards, he had no choice but to ride on toward the ship.

71
00:06:22.057 --> 00:06:29.699
Back at Elsinore, another tragedy was unfolding. After the death of her father, Ophelia had gone insane.

72
00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.430
She wandered past Claudius and Gertrude, who were seated in the great hall.

73
00:06:34.431 --> 00:06:42.191
She was carrying wildflowers, speaking nonsense, and singing bits of sad songs about love and death.

74
00:06:42.192 --> 00:06:46.478
"Grief has made her mad," said Gertrude sadly.

75
00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:52.906
"When sorrows come, they come not as single spies, but in battalions," replied Claudius.

76
00:06:52.907 --> 00:06:58.589
"Hamlet, the man she loved, has murdered her father, and now Hamlet is gone too."

77
00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:01.707
Suddenly they heard footsteps and shouts.

78
00:07:01.708 --> 00:07:05.968
A servant ran into the hall, quickly shutting the doors behind him.

79
00:07:05.969 --> 00:07:11.127
"Laertes has learned of his father's death and returned from France!" he cried.

80
00:07:11.128 --> 00:07:15.231
"The mob calls him ‘lord' and wants to crown him king!"

81
00:07:15.232 --> 00:07:19.778
The doors flew open as Laertes and his followers burst in.

82
00:07:19.779 --> 00:07:26.579
Laertes ran up to Claudius. "I want revenge on the man who killed my father!" he shouted.

83
00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:32.524
"Good Laertes, I am guiltless of your father's death," protested Claudius.

84
00:07:32.525 --> 00:07:37.611
Then Laertes walked up to Ophelia, but she did not recognize her brother.

85
00:07:37.612 --> 00:07:40.771
Instead she handed him some flowers.

86
00:07:40.772 --> 00:07:49.382
"Here," she said. "These are pansies. I wanted violets, but they withered when my father died. Now you must sing!"

87
00:07:49.383 --> 00:07:56.755
Laertes saw that she was mad. "Oh, sweet Ophelia! God in heaven, do you see this?" he cried.

88
00:07:56.756 --> 00:08:03.147
"I share your grief," said the king. "I swear your father's death will be avenged!"

89
00:08:03.148 --> 00:08:07.767
Not long after, a man came to see Horatio with a letter from Hamlet.

90
00:08:07.768 --> 00:08:12.177
The letter explained that Hamlet's ship had been attacked by pirates.

91
00:08:12.178 --> 00:08:22.295
But Hamlet had convinced the pirates to release him, and now he wanted Horatio to meet with him. Hamlet was on his way back to Elsinore!

